{"qas": [
  {"question": "According to NIST AI 600-1, how should organizations address both the measurement of systemic bias in GAI outputs and the documentation of assumptions and limitations of benchmarks?", "answer": "Organizations should apply use-case appropriate benchmarks to quantify systemic bias and document assumptions and limitations of those benchmarks, including possible data contamination.", "evidence": [{"page": 40, "quote": "Apply use-case appropriate benchmarks (e.g., Bias Benchmark Questions, Real Hateful or Harmful Prompts, Winogender Schemas 15 ) to quantify systemic bias, stereotyping, denigration, and hateful content in GAI system outputs; Document assumptions and limitations of benchmarks, including any actual or possible training/test data cross contamination, relative to in-context deployment environment."}, {"page": 40, "quote": "Conduct fairness assessments to measure systemic bias. Measure GAI system performance across demographic groups and subgroups, addressing both quality of service and any allocation of services and resources."}]},
  {"question": "How does NIST AI 600-1 recommend verifying the effectiveness of security measures and tracking the implementation of security incident recommendations?", "answer": "It recommends regularly assessing and verifying security measures remain effective and measuring the rate at which recommendations from security checks and incidents are implemented.", "evidence": [{"page": 37, "quote": "Measure the rate at which recommendations from security checks and incidents are implemented. Assess how quickly the AI system can adapt and improve based on lessons learned from security incidents and feedback."}, {"page": 38, "quote": "Regularly assess and verify that security measures remain effective and have not been compromised."}]},
  {"question": "In NIST AI 600-1, what does 'TEVV' refer to in the context of AI Actor Tasks?", "answer": "'TEVV' refers to Testing, Evaluation, Verification, and Validation, as listed among AI Actor Tasks in multiple tables.", "evidence": [{"page": 33, "quote": "AI Actor Tasks: AI Development, Domain Experts, TEVV"}]},
  {"question": "According to NIST AI 600-1, which action IDs are associated with measuring the reliability of content authentication methods?", "answer": "Action ID MS-2.7-005 is associated with measuring reliability of content authentication methods such as watermarking and cryptographic signatures.", "evidence": [{"page": 37, "quote": "Measure reliability of content authentication methods, such as watermarking, cryptographic signatures, digital fingerprints, as well as access controls, conformity assessment, and model integrity verification, which can help support the effective implementation of content provenance techniques."}]},
  {"question": "Which action in NIST AI 600-1 addresses the need to avoid drawing broad conclusions from limited or anecdotal GAI system assessments?", "answer": "MS-2.5-001 advises to avoid extrapolating GAI system performance or capabilities from narrow, non-systematic, and anecdotal assessments.", "evidence": [{"page": 35, "quote": "Avoid extrapolating GAI system performance or capabilities from narrow, non- systematic, and anecdotal assessments."}]},
  {"question": "What does NIST AI 600-1 suggest for tracking changes to AI-generated content over time to ensure transparency?", "answer": "It suggests using digital content transparency solutions and robust version control systems to document each instance of content generation, modification, or sharing.", "evidence": [{"page": 38, "quote": "Use digital content transparency solutions to enable the documentation of each instance where content is generated, modified, or shared to provide a tamper- proof history of the content, promote transparency, and enable traceability. Robust version control systems can also be applied to track changes across the AI lifecycle over time."}]}
]}
